    {
      "id": "v1/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top1/r03/G3",
      "evidence_sha256": "0898fb74ec2c2ff989cb71de67cf1ea912c8f79d88db86a6bfdc75c11aa8fca3",
      "assessment": "R3 G3 handles signs better: it reads r03 minus r02 as -0.055768, notes Q1/Q2 gains and Q3/Q4 losses, and observes the 20 percent reduction in early displacement. H1 restores [5,5,6,5]; H2 additionally raises the third tick. The final forecast invents a +0.02 to +0.06 recovery range and labels restoration safe despite the dominant -4.36075 Q4 loss. Selected H1 yields 4.164944 in R4.",
      "decision_update": "Preserve direct signed comparison and heterogeneous response analysis. Replace invented gain ranges with qualitative predicted direction plus observable mechanistic and score checks. Compare restoration and extension only when both are actually selected on common parents; do not use sparse score-stratum loss to infer which time window geometrically targets that stratum.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "Restoring the tick vector reproduces the prior mean on the same random setting, but H2 was not sampled and its incremental value is unknown. A low median effect with a large upper-stratum failure is a meaningful risk signal, not evidence that the loss can simply be discarded as noise.",
